id: pm-hipfu9
title: "Recurring reminders: a standing check (daily release-health, weekly governance sweep) re-arms itself on fire instead of requiring manual re-scheduling"
description: "Reminders are single-fire (at=...,text=... only); events already expand recurrence (pm-vg5h) but reminders do not. A standing operational check such as pm-7zs0 (daily auto-release verification) must be re-scheduled by hand after every fire, which means the safety net silently disappears the first time the re-schedule is forgotten. Add an opt-in recurrence field to the reminder facet (cadence + next-fire derivation, re-arm recorded as a history mutation), consistent with the calendar recurrence model and the builtin-calendar package boundary (pm-o3fh). Horizon: near-mid; small surface, high operational value."
type: Feature
status: open
priority: 3
tags[6]: agent-ux,calendar,"horizon:mid",recurrence,release,reminders
created_at: "2026-07-26T22:37:23.410Z"
updated_at: "2026-07-27T12:58:22.730Z"
author: "harness:opencode"
estimated_minutes: 300
acceptance_criteria: "A reminder can declare a recurrence and re-arms itself on fire without manual rescheduling; The re-arm is recorded as a history mutation with the same provenance as any other mutation, so a standing check leaves an auditable trail of every fire; Recurrence semantics match the calendar recurrence model already used by events rather than introducing a second cadence grammar; The existing single-fire behaviour is unchanged when no recurrence is declared; A reminder whose recurrence would fire in the past catches up deterministically or declines explicitly, and the chosen behaviour is documented rather than emergent; pm-7zs0 is converted to a recurring reminder as the acceptance vehicle, proving the operational case that motivated the feature"
parent: pm-mpbb
risk: low
confidence: high
